From: Alex <xnderv@yandex.com>
Subject: [PATCH] LAB drm/nouveau/pmu/gf100: PERF process — GPU load accounting in PMU firmware (step 1: measure; step 2: in-driver governor)

nouveau's gf100 PMU firmware has an empty PERF process. NVIDIA's driver lets its PMU
firmware accumulate the PMU idle/busy counters and the host decides on performance
levels from those totals. Step 1 of doing the same, staged so that each piece can be
switched on separately:

- fuc/perf.fuc: the PERF process is ASLEEP after the firmware loads (no counter writes,
  no timer). PERF_MSG_START(data0 = period in PMU ticks) programs the PMU counters
  exactly as NVIDIA 390 does on GF114 (1 = video, 3 = graphics+copy, 7 = all ticks)
  and samples them every period (31-bit counters wrap in ~10 s, deltas are taken
  modulo 2^31); START(0) puts it back to sleep. PERF_MSG_LOAD returns graphics and
  video load in permille since the previous query (+ sample count) and resets the totals.
- fuc/gf100.fuc3.h regenerated with envyas (before this change the rebuilt header was
  byte-identical to the shipped one).
- gf100.c: lab_perf_ticks (0 = leave the PERF process asleep) and lab_perf_ms
  (0 = no queries); results in /sys/module/nouveau/parameters/lab_perf_{gr,vid,samples}.
  Nothing is reclocked by this part.
- gf100.c, step 2: lab_gov — pstate governor on the PMU load, same rules as the
  gpu-autoclock service (0f at >=50 %, 03->07 at >=15 %, 0f->07 after 15 s <15 %,
  07->03 after 30 s <5 %, 5 s minimum dwell). 0 = off (default), 1 = shadow: only logs
  where it would switch, 2 = switches itself via nvkm_clk_ustate().
- gf100.c, 22.10: temperature brake for the governor (nouveau itself never lowers clocks on
  heat; NVIDIA does). lab_gov_hot: at or above this temperature the governor stays out of 0f
  (and leaves it for 07); lab_gov_crit: drops to 03 and stays; released lab_gov_hyst degrees
  below the threshold. Both 0 by default (= off). lab_gov_temp shows the last reading.
- gf100.c, 24.10: lab_gov_nv — governor timing taken from the NVIDIA 390 trace under load
  (run 7): up straight to 0f on the first sample >= 50 % (NVIDIA was there ~0.25 s after the
  load appeared and never stepped 03->07), down one level after 8 s of low load (0f->07 at
  <15 %, 07->03 at <5 %), no 5 s dwell after a switch. Load thresholds and the temperature
  brake stay ours. 0 by default (= the rules above).


Update 19.11.2026: works without the gpu-autoclock service. Defaults now start sampling and the governor
by themselves: lab_perf_ticks=-1 (100 ms from this card's PMU clock), lab_perf_ms=1000, lab_gov=2;
temperature brake thresholds from the VBIOS sensor table (lab_gov_hot/lab_gov_crit=-1); the VBIOS boot
clocks count as level 07 for the governor's first decision.

21.11: the same PERF process is assembled for the Falcon 4 PMU of GF119 (fuc/gf119.fuc4.h, rebuilt with envyas from the
shared perf.fuc; the unchanged upstream sources rebuild word for word as in the kernel), and GF119 uses the same driver side
(gf100_pmu_init_lab / fini_lab). NVIDIA programs the PMU load counters on GF119 exactly as on GF104–GF116 (4 boards in the
nouveau trace collection). The governor takes its levels from the card's performance table (lowest / middle / top) instead of
03/07/0f: GF119 and GF117 VBIOSes have only 07 and 0f. Untested on GF119 hardware.

22.11: the driver waits at most 1 s for the PERF reply (lab_perf_wait_ms). nouveau's gt215_pmu_send waits forever, and in a
3-hour run (test 114) one request was taken by the PMU firmware but never answered: the governor thread hung for good (no
more clock changes, and suspend would wait for it). Now a lost reply costs one skipped sample, is logged with the state of
both queues, and counted in lab_perf_timeouts. Why the reply was lost is not established (a queue flood from the host does
not reproduce it, test 116). lab_perf_drop / lab_perf_flood are experiment keys.

